answer_C:
When ensuring that a high-risk AI system complies with the requirements laid down in Section 2, the following must be taken into account:

* The system's "intended purpose" [Art. 8(1)];
* The "generally acknowledged state of the art on AI and AI-related technologies" [Art. 8(1)];
* The risk management system referred to in Article 9 [Art. 8(1)].

answer_B:
Where a market surveillance authority takes a decision regarding real-world testing (such as suspending, terminating, or requiring modifications to the testing) or issues an objection to it, the decision or objection must indicate [Art. 76(3)], [Art. 76(4)]:

* The grounds for the decision or objection [Art. 76(4)];
* How the provider or prospective provider can challenge the decision or objection [Art. 76(4)].

The European Data Protection Supervisor may impose administrative fines on Union institutions, bodies, offices, and agencies falling within the scope of the Regulation [Art. 100(1)].

When deciding whether to impose an administrative fine and when deciding on its amount in each individual case, "all relevant circumstances of the specific situation shall be taken into account" and due regard must be given to the following factors:

* **Nature and impact:** The nature, gravity, and duration of the infringement and its consequences, taking into account the purpose of the AI system concerned, as well as, where appropriate, the number of affected persons and the level of damage suffered by them [Art. 100(1)(a)];
* **Responsibility:** The degree of responsibility of the Union institution, body, office, or agency, taking into account technical and organisational measures implemented by them [Art. 100(1)(b)];
* **Mitigation:** Any action taken by the Union institution, body, office, or agency to mitigate the damage suffered by affected persons [Art. 100(1)(c)];
* **Cooperation:** The degree of cooperation with the European Data Protection Supervisor to remedy the infringement and mitigate its possible adverse effects, including compliance with any measures previously ordered against them regarding the same subject matter [Art. 100(1)(d)];
* **Prior conduct:** Any similar previous infringements by the Union institution, body, office, or agency [Art. 100(1)(e)];
* **Notification:** The manner in which the infringement became known to the European Data Protection Supervisor, particularly whether, and to what extent, the institution, body, office, or agency notified the infringement [Art. 100(1)(f)];
* **Budget:** The annual budget of the Union institution, body, office, or agency [Art. 100(1)(g)].

answer_C:
The **European Data Protection Supervisor** may impose administrative fines on Union institutions, bodies, offices, and agencies falling within the scope of the Regulation [Art. 100(1)].

When deciding whether to impose an administrative fine and determining its amount, all relevant circumstances of the specific situation must be taken into account, giving due regard to the following factors [Art. 100(1)]:

* the nature, gravity and duration of the infringement and of its consequences, taking into account the purpose of the AI system concerned, and, where appropriate, the number of affected persons and the level of damage suffered by them [Art. 100(1)(a)];
* the degree of responsibility of the Union institution, body, office or agency, taking into account technical and organisational measures implemented by them [Art. 100(1)(b)];
* any action taken to mitigate the damage suffered by affected persons [Art. 100(1)(c)];
* the degree of cooperation with the European Data Protection Supervisor to remedy the infringement and mitigate its possible adverse effects, including compliance with any previously ordered measures regarding the same subject matter [Art. 100(1)(d)];
* any similar previous infringements by the institution, body, office or agency [Art. 100(1)(e)];
* the manner in which the infringement became known to the European Data Protection Supervisor, in particular whether and to what extent it was notified [Art. 100(1)(f)];
* the annual budget of the Union institution, body, office or agency [Art. 100(1)(g)].

answer_A:
Yes. Under the EU AI Act, notified bodies are required to share assessment results with other notified bodies carrying out similar activities covering the same types of AI systems [Art. 45(3)].

Specifically, each notified body must:
* Provide those other notified bodies with "relevant information on issues relating to negative and, on request, positive conformity assessment results" [Art. 45(3)];
* Inform other notified bodies of quality management system approvals that it has "refused, suspended or withdrawn, and, upon request, of quality system approvals which it has issued" [Art. 45(2)(a)];
* Inform other notified bodies of Union technical documentation assessment certificates (or supplements) that it has "refused, withdrawn, suspended or otherwise restricted, and, upon request, of the certificates and/or supplements thereto which it has issued" [Art. 45(2)(b)].

answer_C:
The **Commission** can update the rules when technology changes by adopting delegated acts to amend:

*   The thresholds, benchmarks and indicators for classifying general-purpose AI models with systemic risk "in light of evolving technological developments" [Art. 51(3)];
*   The criteria in Annex XIII for designating models as presenting systemic risks [Art. 52(4)];
*   Annexes XI and XII (technical documentation and information for downstream providers) "in light of evolving technological developments" [Art. 53(6)].

answer_A:
A general-purpose AI model is classified as having systemic risk if it meets either of the conditions in Article 51(1): (a) it has **high impact capabilities** evaluated on the basis of appropriate technical tools and methodologies, including indicators and benchmarks, or (b) the Commission designates it (ex officio or following a qualified alert from the scientific panel) as having capabilities or an impact **equivalent** to those in point (a), having regard to the criteria set out in Annex XIII [Art. 51(1)].

The specific factors listed in Annex XIII for determining equivalent capabilities or impact are:
*   the **number of parameters** of the model;
*   the **quality or size of the data set** (e.g., measured through tokens);
*   the **amount of computation** used for training (measured in floating point operations, or indicated by estimated cost, time, or energy consumption);
*   the **input and output modalities** (e.g., text-to-text, text-to-image, multi-modality) and state-of-the-art thresholds for high-impact capabilities for each modality, and specific types of inputs/outputs;
*   the **benchmarks and evaluations** of capabilities, including number of tasks without additional training, adaptability, autonomy, scalability, and tools accessed;
*   **high impact on the internal market due to reach**, presumed when made available to at least **10 000 registered business users** established in the Union;
*   the **number of registered end-users** [Annex XIII].

Additionally, a model is **presumed** to have high impact capabilities when the cumulative computation used for its training exceeds **10^25 floating point operations** [Art. 51(2)].

answer_B:
A general-purpose AI model is classified as a general-purpose AI model with systemic risk if it meets either of the following conditions [Art. 51(1)]:

* **High impact capabilities:** Evaluated on the basis of appropriate technical tools and methodologies, including indicators and benchmarks [Art. 51(1)(a)]. A model is presumed to meet this condition when the cumulative amount of computation used for its training exceeds $10^{25}$ floating point operations [Art. 51(2)].
* **Commission designation:** Decided by the Commission (ex officio or following a qualified alert from the scientific panel) because the model has capabilities or an impact equivalent to high impact capabilities, assessed against the criteria in Annex XIII [Art. 51(1)(b)], [Art. 52(4)].

Under Annex XIII, the criteria considered for determining equivalent capabilities or impact are [Annex XIII]:
* **Parameters:** The number of parameters of the model [Annex XIII(a)];
* **Data set:** The quality or size of the data set, such as measured through tokens [Annex XIII(b)];
* **Computation:** The amount of computation used for training, measured in floating point operations or indicated by variables such as estimated training cost, time required, or energy consumption [Annex XIII(c)];
* **Modalities:** The input and output modalities (e.g., text-to-text, text-to-image, multi-modality), state-of-the-art thresholds for each modality, and specific input/output types (e.g., biological sequences) [Annex XIII(d)];
* **Capability benchmarks and evaluations:** Factors including the number of tasks performed without additional training, adaptability to learn new and distinct tasks, level of autonomy, scalability, and tools the model can access [Annex XIII(e)];
* **Market reach:** Whether the model has a high impact on the internal market due to reach, which is presumed if made available to at least 10,000 registered business users established in the Union [Annex XIII(f)];
* **End-users:** The number of registered end-users [Annex XIII(g)].

answer_C:
A general-purpose AI model is classified as presenting a systemic risk if it meets either of the following conditions:

* **High impact capabilities:** Evaluated on the basis of appropriate technical tools and methodologies, including indicators and benchmarks [Art. 51(1)(a)]. A model is **presumed** to have high impact capabilities when the cumulative amount of computation used for its training measured in floating point operations is greater than $10^{25}$ [Art. 51(2)].
* **Commission decision (equivalent capabilities or impact):** Based on a decision of the Commission (ex officio or following a qualified alert from the scientific panel), when it has capabilities or an impact equivalent to high impact capabilities, having regard to specific criteria [Art. 51(1)(b)], [Art. 52(4)].

For determining equivalent capabilities or impact, the Commission takes into account the following criteria set out in Annex XIII [Art. 51(1)(b)], [Annex XIII]:
* The number of parameters of the model [Annex XIII, point (a)];
* The quality or size of the data set (for example, measured through tokens) [Annex XIII, point (b)];
* The amount of computation used for training the model, measured in floating point operations or indicated by a combination of variables such as estimated cost, estimated time, or estimated energy consumption for training [Annex XIII, point (c)];
* The input and output modalities (such as text to text, text to image, multi-modality), state-of-the-art thresholds for determining high-impact capabilities for each modality, and specific input/output types (e.g., biological sequences) [Annex XIII, point (d)];
* Benchmarks and capability evaluations, including the number of tasks performed without additional training, adaptability to learn new and distinct tasks, level of autonomy, scalability, and access to tools [Annex XIII, point (e)];
* Whether it has a high impact on the internal market due to reach, which is presumed when made available to at least 10,000 registered business users established in the Union [Annex XIII, point (f)];
* The number of registered end-users [Annex XIII, point (g)].

In general, systemic risk is defined by whether the model's high-impact capabilities have a significant impact on the Union market due to reach, or actual or reasonably foreseeable negative effects on public health, safety, public security, fundamental rights, or society as a whole, propagating at scale across the value chain [Art. 3(65)].

answer_A:
Upon a request by national competent authorities, providers of high-risk AI systems must provide or make accessible the following documentation and information:

* **Conformity information and documentation:** All the information and documentation necessary to demonstrate the conformity of the high-risk AI system with the requirements set out in Section 2, provided upon a reasoned request in a language easily understood by the authority in one of the official languages of the Union institutions indicated by the Member State concerned [Art. 21(1)], [Art. 16(k)].
* **EU declaration of conformity:** A copy of the written EU declaration of conformity must be submitted upon request [Art. 47(1)]. Providers must also keep this declaration at the disposal of national competent authorities for 10 years after the system has been placed on the market or put into service [Art. 47(1)], [Art. 18(1)(e)].
* **Automatically generated logs:** Access to the automatically generated logs referred to in Article 12(1), upon a reasoned request, to the extent such logs are under their control [Art. 21(2)].
* **Documentation kept at disposal:** For a period ending 10 years after the system has been placed on the market or put into service, providers must keep at the disposal of national competent authorities [Art. 18(1)]:
  * The technical documentation referred to in Article 11 [Art. 18(1)(a)];
  * Documentation concerning the quality management system referred to in Article 17 [Art. 18(1)(b)];
  * Documentation concerning changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
  * Decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
  * The EU declaration of conformity referred to in Article 47 [Art. 18(1)(e)].

answer_C:
Upon request by a competent authority, providers of high-risk AI systems must make available:

* **Conformity information and documentation:** All information and documentation necessary to demonstrate the conformity of the system with the requirements set out in Section 2, upon a reasoned request [Art. 21(1)], [Art. 16(k)]. This must be provided in a language easily understood by the authority, in one of the official languages of the Union institutions as indicated by the Member State concerned [Art. 21(1)].
* **Automatically generated logs:** Access to the automatically generated logs of the AI system referred to in Article 12(1), upon a reasoned request and as applicable, to the extent such logs are under the provider's control [Art. 21(2)].
* **EU declaration of conformity:** A copy of the written machine-readable, physical, or electronically signed EU declaration of conformity upon request [Art. 47(1)].

Additionally, providers must keep the following at the disposal of national competent authorities for 10 years after the system is placed on the market or put into service [Art. 18(1)], [Art. 47(1)]:
* Technical documentation [Art. 18(1)(a)];
* Quality management system documentation [Art. 18(1)(b)];
* Documentation on changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
* Decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
* The EU declaration of conformity [Art. 18(1)(e)], [Art. 47(1)].

Under Article 66(j), the Board assists national competent authorities and the Commission by:

* Assisting them in "developing the organisational and technical expertise required for the implementation of this Regulation" [Art. 66(j)];
* "contributing to the assessment of training needs for staff of Member States involved in implementing this Regulation" [Art. 66(j)].

answer_A:
Yes. Notifying authorities are required to safeguard the confidentiality of the information that they obtain, in accordance with Article 78 [Art. 28(6)]. 

Under these rules, entities involved in the application of the Regulation must respect the confidentiality of information and data in accordance with Union or national law, specifically to protect:
* intellectual property rights and confidential business information or trade secrets (including source code), subject to specific exceptions under Directive (EU) 2016/943 [Art. 78(1)(a)];
* the effective implementation of the Regulation, particularly for inspections, investigations, or audits [Art. 78(1)(b)];
* public and national security interests [Art. 78(1)(c)];
* the conduct of criminal or administrative proceedings [Art. 78(1)(d)];
* information classified under Union or national law [Art. 78(1)(e)].

However, when requested, the notifying authority must provide the Commission with all relevant information relating to the notification or the maintenance of the competence of the notified body concerned [Art. 37(2)].

answer_C:
Yes. Notifying authorities are required to safeguard the confidentiality of the information that they obtain, in accordance with Article 78 [Art. 28(6)]. 

Under these rules, confidentiality of information and data obtained in carrying out tasks and activities must be respected in accordance with Union or national law, in particular to protect [Art. 78(1)]:
* intellectual property rights and confidential business information or trade secrets of a natural or legal person, including source code (except in cases referred to in Article 5 of Directive (EU) 2016/943) [Art. 78(1)(a)];
* the effective implementation of the Regulation, particularly for inspections, investigations, or audits [Art. 78(1)(b)];
* public and national security interests [Art. 78(1)(c)];
* the conduct of criminal or administrative proceedings [Art. 78(1)(d)];
* information classified pursuant to Union or national law [Art. 78(1)(e)].

However, the notifying authority is required to provide the Commission, on request, with all relevant information relating to the notification or the maintenance of the competence of the notified body concerned [Art. 37(2)]. (The Commission must likewise ensure that sensitive information obtained during such investigations is treated confidentially [Art. 37(3)]).

Notifying authorities notify the Commission and other Member States of conformity assessment bodies using the electronic notification tool developed and managed by the Commission [Art. 30(2)].

The notification must include:
* Full details of the conformity assessment activities [Art. 30(3)];
* The conformity assessment module or modules [Art. 30(3)];
* The types of AI systems concerned [Art. 30(3)];
* The relevant attestation of competence [Art. 30(3)].

Where the notification is not based on an accreditation certificate as referred to in Article 29(2), the notifying authority must also provide documentary evidence attesting to the competence of the body and the arrangements in place to ensure regular monitoring and continued compliance with Article 31 [Art. 30(3)].

The conformity assessment body may only perform the activities of a notified body if no objections are raised by the Commission or other Member States within:
* Two weeks, where the notification includes an accreditation certificate [Art. 30(4)]; or
* Two months, where it includes documentary evidence [Art. 30(4)].

answer_A:
Under the AI Act, ‘AI literacy’ means "skills, knowledge and understanding that allow providers, deployers and affected persons, taking into account their respective rights and obligations in the context of this Regulation, to make an informed deployment of AI systems, as well as to gain awareness about the opportunities and risks of AI and possible harm it can cause" [Art. 3(56)].

Recital 20 further clarifies that AI literacy should equip these parties with the notions needed to "make informed decisions regarding AI systems," which can include [Recital 20]:
* understanding the correct application of technical elements during the development phase [Recital 20];
* understanding the measures to apply during use and the suitable ways to interpret the AI system's output [Recital 20];
* for affected persons, the knowledge necessary to understand how decisions assisted by AI will impact them [Recital 20]; and
* providing actors across the AI value chain with the insights needed to ensure appropriate compliance and enforcement [Recital 20].

Under the EU AI Act, individuals assigned to human oversight of high-risk AI systems must be natural persons who possess:

* "the necessary competence, training and authority, as well as the necessary support" [Art. 26(2)], [Recital 73].

Additionally, the system must be provided to the deployer in a manner that enables these individuals, as appropriate and proportionate, to:
* properly understand the system's relevant capacities and limitations and duly monitor its operation, including detecting and addressing anomalies, dysfunctions, and unexpected performance [Art. 14(4)(a)];
* remain aware of the tendency to automatically rely or over-rely on the output (automation bias) [Art. 14(4)(b)];
* correctly interpret the system’s output [Art. 14(4)(c)];
* decide not to use the system or disregard, override, or reverse its output [Art. 14(4)(d)];
* intervene in the operation or interrupt the system to bring it to a halt in a safe state [Art. 14(4)(e)].

answer_B:
No, not just any team member can monitor or oversee a high-risk AI system. The AI Act sets specific criteria for the individuals assigned to these tasks:

* **General human oversight (deployers):** Deployers must assign human oversight specifically to natural persons who have the "necessary competence, training and authority, as well as the necessary support" [Art. 26(2)]. 
* **Operational capabilities:** The individuals assigned to oversee the system must be enabled to:
  * Properly understand the system's capacities and limitations and duly monitor its operation (detecting anomalies, dysfunctions, and unexpected performance) [Art. 14(4)(a)];
  * Remain aware of automation bias (the tendency to automatically rely or over-rely on system output) [Art. 14(4)(b)];
  * Correctly interpret the system's output [Art. 14(4)(c)];
  * Decide to disregard, override, reverse, or not use the system [Art. 14(4)(d)];
  * Intervene or halt the system safely via a "stop" button or similar procedure [Art. 14(4)(e)].
* **Monitoring obligations:** Deployers must monitor the AI system's operation specifically "on the basis of the instructions for use" [Art. 26(5)].
* **Real-world testing oversight (if applicable):** If testing in real-world conditions outside sandboxes, oversight must be conducted by individuals who are "suitably qualified in the relevant field and have the necessary capacity, training and authority to perform their tasks" [Art. 60(4)(j)].

This explanation is based on the statutory criteria and does not constitute legal advice.

answer_C:
You do not have to repeat the same work if it has already been addressed:

* **Complementary relationship:** If any of the obligations for the fundamental rights impact assessment (FRIA) are already met through a data protection impact assessment (DPIA) conducted pursuant to Article 35 of Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR) or Article 27 of Directive (EU) 2016/680, the FRIA **shall complement** that DPIA rather than duplicate it [Art. 27(4)].
* **Remaining FRIA elements:** The deployer must still ensure that all elements required under Article 27(1) are fulfilled. To the extent they are not covered by the DPIA, the assessment must include:
  * a description of the deployer's processes in which the system will be used according to its intended purpose [Art. 27(1)(a)];
  * the period of time and frequency of intended use [Art. 27(1)(b)];
  * the categories of natural persons and groups likely to be affected [Art. 27(1)(c)];
  * specific risks of harm to those persons or groups, taking into account information provided under Article 13 [Art. 27(1)(d)];
  * the implementation of human oversight measures [Art. 27(1)(e)];
  * measures to be taken if risks materialise, including governance arrangements and complaint mechanisms [Art. 27(1)(f)].
* **Relying on prior assessments:** In similar cases, deployers may also rely on previously conducted FRIAs or existing impact assessments carried out by the provider [Art. 27(2)].
* **Notification and registration:** Following the assessment, deployers must notify the market surveillance authority of the results using the AI Office template [Art. 27(3), Art. 27(5)]. In addition, where registration under Article 49(3) applies, deployers must provide both a summary of the FRIA findings and a summary of the DPIA carried out under the GDPR or Directive (EU) 2016/680 [Annex VIII, Section C, points 4–5].

This is not legal advice.

answer_B:
According to the provided excerpts, the participants in the standardisation process must seek to enhance multi-stakeholder governance to ensure:

* A balanced representation of interests [Art. 40(3)];
* The effective participation of all relevant stakeholders in accordance with Articles 5, 6, and 7 of Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012 [Art. 40(3)].

answer_A:
The provided provisions do not address who inherits a person's AI digital twin after they die. 

The provided excerpts instead cover:
* Definitions of an ‘AI system’ [Art. 3(1)], ‘provider’ [Art. 3(3)], ‘deployer’ [Art. 3(4)], ‘authorised representative’ [Art. 3(5)], ‘intended purpose’ [Art. 3(12)], and ‘serious incident’ (which includes an incident leading to the death of a person) [Art. 3(49)];
* Timeframes for reporting a serious incident in the event of the death of a person [Art. 73(4)];
* Requirements concerning the EU declaration of conformity for high-risk AI systems [Art. 47(1)].

Yes. Providers of high-risk AI systems are required to put a quality management system in place that ensures compliance with the Regulation [Art. 17(1)(a)]. 

This system must be documented "in a systematic and orderly manner in the form of written policies, procedures and instructions" [Art. 17(1)(a)], and it must cover at least the following aspects:

* A strategy for regulatory compliance, including conformity assessment procedures and management of modifications [Art. 17(1)(a)];
* Techniques, procedures, and systematic actions for the design, design control, and design verification of the system [Art. 17(1)(b)];
* Techniques, procedures, and systematic actions for development, quality control, and quality assurance [Art. 17(1)(c)];
* Systems and procedures for data management (such as data collection, labelling, storage, retention, etc.) performed before and for placing on the market or putting into service [Art. 17(1)(f)];
* The risk management system referred to in Article 9 [Art. 17(1)(g)];
* Setting up, implementing, and maintaining a post-market monitoring system in accordance with Article 72 [Art. 17(1)(h)];
* Systems and procedures for record-keeping of relevant documentation and information [Art. 17(1)(k)];
* An accountability framework detailing the responsibilities of management and staff regarding all of these aspects [Art. 17(1)(m)].

answer_B:
Yes. Providers of high-risk AI systems are required to put in place a quality management system that ensures compliance with the Regulation [Art. 17(1)(a)]. 

This system must be "documented in a systematic and orderly manner in the form of written policies, procedures and instructions" and must include at least the following elements [Art. 17(1)(a)]:

* A strategy for regulatory compliance, covering conformity assessment procedures and management of modifications [Art. 17(1)(a)];
* Techniques, procedures, and systematic actions for the design, design control, and design verification of the system [Art. 17(1)(b)];
* Techniques, procedures, and systematic actions for development, quality control, and quality assurance [Art. 17(1)(c)];
* Systems and procedures for data management (such as data collection, labelling, storage, and retention prior to placing on the market or putting into service) [Art. 17(1)(f)];
* The risk management system referred to in Article 9 [Art. 17(1)(g)];
* The setting-up, implementation, and maintenance of a post-market monitoring system in accordance with Article 72 [Art. 17(1)(h)];
* Systems and procedures for record-keeping of relevant documentation and information [Art. 17(1)(k)];
* An accountability framework establishing the responsibilities of management and staff [Art. 17(1)(m)].
